{
  "dom_id": "step-q303064",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=k?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}-8}{x-2}, & x<k \\\\ -2k-x, & x\\ge k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nArranged in **ascending order**,\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,k=$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "From the definition of $g(x)$, the left-sided limit of $g(x)$ at $x=k$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{3}-8}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)({x}^{2}+2x+4)}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}({x}^{2}+2x+4)}{\\cancel{x-2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}({x}^{2}+2x+4) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+2k+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {k}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{+}}(-2k-x) \\\\  & =-2k-k \\\\  & =-3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $g(k)=-3k.$ \n\nSo, for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=k,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ {k}^{2}+2k+4 & =-3k \\\\ {k}^{2}+5k+4 & =0 \\\\ (k+4)(k+1) & =0 \\\\ k & =-4,\\,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous at $x=k$ for the values\n\n$$\nk=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,k=-1.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
